<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/minerals-and-crystals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Minerals are <strong>nature's chemical compounds with a structural discipline</strong>:
    fixed recipes, atomic lattices, and physical properties that follow directly from the
    structure. Learning to read a mineral — hardness, cleavage, lustre — is learning chemistry
    you can do with a pocket knife and a streak plate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the recipe, the lattice, the diagnostic tests, the families" widget="equationStep" config={mineralCfg} />

  <h2>Minerals as records and resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minerals remember their formation:</strong> the chemistry and inclusions of a crystal record pressure, temperature and fluid conditions — geologists read the growth history from a single grain (zircon crystals older than 4.4 billion years are Earth\'s oldest dated material).</li>
    <li><strong>Economic geology:</strong> ores are unusual mineral concentrations — banded iron formations (ancient ocean chemistry), porphyry copper deposits (hydrothermal concentration), lithium brines (evaporated basins). Every battery and wind turbine starts as a mineral deposit.</li>
    <li><strong>Crystallography birthed X-ray science:</strong> von Laue and the Braggs (Nobel 1914–15) used crystals as diffraction gratings to discover X-ray wavelengths — and accidentally opened the door to DNA structure (the biology lesson\'s Photo 51).</li>
    <li><strong>Gems are geology plus scarcity:</strong> diamond (mantle pressure), ruby (chromium in corundum), emerald (beryllium + chromium + vanadium in the right rock) — value tracks rarity of conditions, not hardness alone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Klein & Hurlbut, Manual of Mineralogy; USGS mineral commodity summaries; Valley et al. on Hadean zircons (2015).</p>
</TopicPage>
